return [
    18 => [
        'overview' => 'Provider Accreditation Program is the complete path for input and service providers seeking accredited status. It covers the accreditation pathway, business verification, coverage and capability, quality systems, marketplace conduct, audit preparation, and maintaining accreditation in good standing.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Accreditation Pathway and Standards',
                'summary' => 'The steps, standards, and timeline to accreditation.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Accreditation is the platform's mark of a verified, trustworthy provider. The pathway is a defined sequence, and knowing it in advance turns a daunting process into a checklist you can execute.\n\nThe pathway begins with registration: your business profile, contacts, and base of operations. It continues with verification: your registration documents, tax identifier, and the evidence that your business is real and reachable. Finally comes accreditation: the platform's confirmation that you meet the provider standards.\n\nThe standards have four pillars. First, legitimacy: a real, registered, reachable business. Second, capability: the coverage, stock, or skill you claim. Third, quality: listings and services that match their descriptions. Fourth, conduct: honest pricing, truthful claims, and platform-compliant behaviour. Each pillar has observable criteria, and the review checks all four.\n\nUnderstand the timeline. Verification of documents typically completes within a few business days when the evidence is clear; accreditation follows once the profile, coverage, and listings meet the standards. Incomplete submissions are the main delay, so prepare everything before you start.\n\nFinally, treat accreditation as a status to keep, not a prize to win. Accredited providers are reviewed periodically and can be suspended for conduct or quality failures. The provider who treats the standards as daily practice keeps the badge permanently.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Business Verification and Documentation',
                'summary' => 'The documents that prove your business is real.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Verification exists to protect buyers: it confirms that the provider they are buying from is a real business with a real address and a real owner. Your documentation is the evidence, and preparing it well is the fastest route through review.\n\nCore documents are your business registration or its recognised equivalent, your tax identifier where applicable, and identification for the owner or authorised representative. Keep digital scans or clear photos of each, with all four corners visible and the text legible.\n\nContact evidence matters as much as paper. Reviewers check that the phone is answered, the email is monitored, and the base of operations exists. A depot photo, a market stall photo, or a signed letter from a landlord or market association all work as location evidence.\n\nConsistency is the silent requirement. The business name on the profile, the registration document, the bank account, and the invoices should all match. Mismatches are not automatically fatal, but they trigger manual checks that add days to the review.\n\nFinally, keep the file current. When a document expires, a registration changes, or you move depot, update the profile promptly. Providers whose records are current sail through periodic re-reviews; those whose records drift are suspended while they chase paperwork.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Coverage and Service Capability',
                'summary' => 'Declare and prove what you can genuinely deliver.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Coverage and capability are what buyers actually buy: the ability to deliver a product or service, in a specific place, at a specific time. Accreditation requires that your declared coverage matches your real capability, and that you can evidence both.\n\nDeclare your true coverage first. The states and LGAs where you genuinely operate, with stock, staff, or delivery, are your coverage. Declaring more than you serve creates unfulfilled orders, cancellations, and a falling reliability score; declaring less than you serve merely limits your visibility, which you can grow later.\n\nEvidence your capability. For product providers: supplier agreements, invoices, stock records, and depot or warehouse photos. For service providers: the equipment, qualifications, and team that deliver the service, with photos or certificates where available. The review sample-checks listings against this evidence, so keep it organised and retrievable.\n\nPlan lead times honestly. If remote LGAs take longer, say so in your listings. Buyers weigh delivery time against need, and an honest two-week delivery beats a promised two-day delivery that quietly becomes two weeks.\n\nFinally, update coverage when reality changes. New depots, new routes, and seasonal availability all belong in the profile. The provider whose declared coverage always matches reality is the provider buyers trust with repeat orders.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Product and Service Quality Systems',
                'summary' => 'Systems that keep quality consistent, job after job.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Quality is not an intention; it is a system. Accredited providers run simple systems that keep their products and services consistent, because consistency is what accreditation promises to buyers.\n\nFor product providers, the quality system starts at intake: check incoming stock against the order, verify labels and registration, and reject anything damaged, expired, or unlabelled. Then control storage: separate, secure, ventilated, and rotated by expiry. These two steps prevent most quality failures before a product reaches a buyer.\n\nFor service providers, the system is the written method: the standard steps for each core job, the safety requirements, and the quality bar at completion. Train every team member to the method, and use the job checklist so nothing is skipped. Two different teams producing the same result is the hallmark of a real system.\n\nHandle defects deliberately. When a product or job falls short, record it, fix it, and note the cause. A provider who quietly absorbs defects repeats them; one who records them eliminates the causes over time.\n\nFinally, ask for feedback and act on it. Buyer reviews are free quality audits. The accredited provider reads reviews as data, fixes the causes, and watches the rating recover. That loop, running monthly, is the quality system that keeps accreditation meaningful.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Marketplace Conduct and Trust',
                'summary' => 'The conduct rules that keep the marketplace fair.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The marketplace works because buyers trust it, and trust is enforced by conduct rules. Accredited providers are held to the highest standard, because their badge is a promise to every buyer who sees it.\n\nPricing conduct comes first. List real prices you can honour, update them when the market moves, and never bait buyers with a low price you intend to negotiate upward. Bait pricing is a violation that can suspend a listing, and it poisons buyer trust far beyond the one product.\n\nClaims conduct is next. Describe products accurately and within what the label or registration supports. Exaggerated claims about yield, speed, or safety are violations of both marketplace policy and product regulation, and they endanger the farmers who rely on them.\n\nTransaction conduct keeps the platform protective. Payments stay inside the platform, orders are confirmed only when fulfilled as described, and disputes are handled with evidence rather than argument. Off-platform payments remove buyer protection and are a serious violation.\n\nFinally, treat reviews as a responsibility, not a nuisance. Respond professionally to criticism, fix the cause where it is real, and never pressure buyers to change honest feedback. The provider whose conduct earns a strong rating is the provider the marketplace surfaces to more buyers.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Accreditation Audit Preparation',
                'summary' => 'Be ready for the sample-check, every time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Accreditation is verified by sample-checks: reviewers pick listings, coverage claims, or documents and compare them against reality. Preparation is the discipline of being audit-ready every day, so a sample-check is a formality rather than a crisis.\n\nKeep the evidence file current and retrievable. Registration, tax identifier, product files with labels and invoices, coverage evidence, and service records should be findable in minutes. When a reviewer asks, the answer that arrives same-day closes the check; the answer that arrives a week later reads as a failure.\n\nWalk your own listings as a buyer. Search for your products, open each listing, and check the title, category, price, and stock against your actual inventory. Fix discrepancies before a reviewer finds them, because a listing that contradicts your own records is the classic sample-check failure.\n\nVerify coverage claims periodically. Confirm that each declared LGA still has stock or service, and remove the ones that do not. A coverage claim that fails a spot-check damages the whole accreditation, not just the one listing.\n\nFinally, prepare the team. Everyone who answers the phone or handles orders should know the profile, the coverage, and the product basics. A reviewer calling an unanswered phone or an uninformed staff member is a failed check that took two minutes to prevent.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Maintaining and Renewing Accreditation',
                'summary' => 'Keep the badge through continuous compliance.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Accreditation is a renewable status, and the renewal is easy only for providers who kept the standards all along. Maintenance is the discipline of continuous compliance, periodic review, and timely renewal.\n\nMonitor your own compliance monthly. Run a short internal audit: documents current, listings accurate, stock honest, coverage real, and no conduct warnings. Fix any gap immediately, and record the fix. The monthly habit makes the periodic review a non-event.\n\nTrack the renewal date. Accreditation lapses when renewal is missed, and a lapsed badge costs visibility and buyer trust. Put the renewal on the calendar with a reminder a month ahead, so a busy season never swallows it.\n\nRespond to platform requests promptly. Clarification requests, document refresh requests, and sample-checks all have deadlines; the providers who answer within a day are the ones whose accreditation stays uninterrupted. Silence reads as an inactive or unreachable business.\n\nFinally, grow the accreditation deliberately. New product lines, new coverage, or new services should be added through the same verification discipline, not by simply editing the profile. The provider who treats every change as part of the accreditation keeps a badge that buyers, and the platform, can rely on for years.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Accreditation Pathway Checklist', 'type' => 'checklist', 'notes' => 'Registration, verification, and accreditation steps.'],
            ['title' => 'Business Verification File', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Registration, tax ID, ID, contacts, and location evidence.'],
            ['title' => 'Capability Evidence Pack', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Stock, supplier, depot, and service capability evidence.'],
            ['title' => 'Monthly Compliance Audit', 'type' => 'checklist', 'notes' => 'Documents, listings, coverage, conduct, and renewal.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Provider Accreditation Program Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What are the four pillars of the provider standards?', 'a' => 'Price, speed, size, and age', 'b' => 'Legitimacy, capability, quality, and conduct', 'c' => 'Marketing, branding, photos, and discounts', 'd' => 'Friends, family, staff, and suppliers', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why must profile, document, and bank names match?', 'a' => 'Mismatches trigger manual checks and delay accreditation', 'b' => 'The platform charges more otherwise', 'c' => 'Buyers prefer long names', 'd' => 'It is a design preference only', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'What is the risk of declaring coverage you cannot serve?', 'a' => 'Nothing; coverage claims are free advertising', 'b' => 'Unfulfilled orders, cancellations, and a falling reliability score', 'c' => 'The platform auto-corrects it', 'd' => 'It only affects the one listing', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'How should a provider prepare for a sample-check?', 'a' => 'Delete anything that might be questioned', 'b' => 'Keep the evidence file current and walk listings as a buyer', 'c' => 'Only prepare after the reviewer calls', 'd' => 'Ask the reviewer which items will be checked', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What happens if accreditation renewal is missed?', 'a' => 'Nothing; accreditation is permanent', 'b' => 'The badge lapses, costing visibility and buyer trust', 'c' => 'The account is deleted immediately', 'd' => 'Only new listings are affected', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    19 => [
        'overview' => 'Field Agent Certification Program certifies agents to register growers, collect farm data, capture GPS and photo evidence, support program enrollment, and build community trust. It is the required certification before conducting unsupervised field work.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'The Certified Field Agent Mandate',
                'summary' => 'What certification authorises you to do.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Certification is the platform's statement that you are qualified to represent it in the field. Understanding the mandate, its scope, and its limits is the first lesson of the program, and it governs everything that follows.\n\nYour mandate is to bring the platform to the field: register growers accurately, verify farms with defensible evidence, support program enrollment, and be the trusted face of the cooperative. You are the bridge between the grower's reality and the platform's records.\n\nThe mandate has boundaries. You collect, verify, and report; you do not approve, promise outcomes, or handle funds. When a grower asks whether their application will be approved, the honest answer is that the verification team decides on the evidence you collect. Promising outcomes you cannot control damages the grower's trust and the platform's integrity.\n\nCertification is earned, not assumed. It requires completing this program, passing the assessment, and conducting supervised field work until your evidence meets the standard unaided. Until then, work under a senior agent's review.\n\nFinally, carry the mandate with pride and humility. Growers will trust you with their names, their farms, and sometimes their hopes. The certified agent who honours that trust is the platform's most valuable asset in every LGA.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Grower Registration and Onboarding',
                'summary' => 'Register growers accurately, the first time.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Registration is the grower's entry to the platform, and accuracy at this moment determines everything downstream: payments, programs, and verification. The certified agent's skill is getting it right the first time.\n\nStart with identity. Record the grower's legal name as shown on their ID, and note any variation you hear. Confirm the phone number they control, because OTPs and wallet alerts depend on it, and the email if they have one. A shared family phone is a data risk; flag it and help the grower plan for their own SIM.\n\nCapture location precisely. State and LGA from the official dropdowns, never free text, because reporting aggregates on those exact values. For the farm itself, record the identifiable point: entrance, farmhouse, or named landmark, and the GPS coordinate to match.\n\nRecord the farm facts: area, main crops, stand count, and any intercropping. Estimates are acceptable at registration, but note that they are estimates; the cooperative refines them after the first harvest and field visit.\n\nFinally, hand the grower their next steps: what happens to their record, when to expect verification, and how to reach support. A grower who knows the process is a grower who completes it. The agent who explains is the agent whose registrations stay clean.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Farm Data Collection Standards',
                'summary' => 'Collect data that survives audit and drives decisions.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Field data is the raw material of every platform decision: coverage maps, program targeting, and impact reports. Collecting it to standard means it survives audit and actually drives those decisions.\n\nLearn the core data standards. Names are recorded as on the ID; locations come from the official hierarchy and a GPS fix; quantities are counted or measured, not guessed where counting is possible; and every record carries a date and the collector's identity. Standards exist so that two agents visiting the same farm record the same facts.\n\nUse the right tool for each data type. The platform's capture flow timestamps GPS and links it to the visit; use it rather than a notes app, because the platform's record is the one the verification team reviews. Free-text notes should be factual and specific: 'three dead palms, block two' beats 'farm looks poor'.\n\nHandle edge cases honestly. A farm without a clear entrance, a grower without an ID, a shared plot within a larger estate: each has a documented way to record it. When evidence is impossible, record the obstacle precisely; an honest limitation is data, a guess is an error.\n\nFinally, submit the same day. Fresh records are accurate records, and same-day submission keeps the verification queue moving. The agent whose data is complete, precise, and prompt is the agent whose work is trusted at review.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'GPS and Photo Evidence Mastery',
                'summary' => 'Evidence that proves the visit happened and matched.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "GPS and photos are the two halves of field evidence: the coordinate proves where you were, and the photo proves what was there. Mastery means producing evidence that a reviewer, far away, can trust without a single follow-up question.\n\nCapture the GPS fix properly. Stand at the farm's identifiable point in open sky, hold still for fifteen to thirty seconds, and let the phone settle on a stable fix. A moving or indoor fix can be off by hundreds of metres, which misplaces the farm and delays verification.\n\nTie the coordinate to a landmark photo. A photo of the entrance sign, farmhouse, or distinctive tree, taken from the same spot, makes the coordinate meaningful. Avoid generic field photos that could be anywhere; the landmark is what makes the evidence defensible.\n\nBuild the standard photo set: the identifying landmark, the farm itself, the palms or depot, and any declared features such as intercropping or a nursery. Shoot in daylight, frame the subject, and caption each photo factually. Include people only with consent.\n\nNever fabricate, reuse, or guess. An old photo, a downloaded image, or an invented coordinate is an integrity breach that ends a career. When conditions prevent good evidence, record the limitation honestly and let the review decide. Precise, honest, complete evidence is the certified agent's signature.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Program Enrollment and Support',
                'summary' => 'Guide growers into the right programs.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Programs are how the platform delivers value to growers: input schemes, training, financing, and market access. Your role is to guide eligible growers into the right programs, accurately and without favouritism.\n\nKnow the program landscape. For each active program in your state, know the target audience, the eligibility rules, the benefit, and the enrollment path. Programs for young palms, for verified growers, or for specific crops each need different lists; targeting the wrong growers wastes the program and damages trust.\n\nBuild the eligible list from the registry, not from memory. The registry tells you who is verified, what they grow, and where; the program rules tell you who qualifies. A list built from data is defensible; a list built from friends is a scandal waiting to happen.\n\nExplain the program honestly. State what the grower receives, what is expected of them, the timeline, and the fact that approval depends on verification. Do not promise outcomes or charge anything for enrollment; programs are free to eligible growers, and anyone charging is committing fraud.\n\nFinally, follow up. Enrollment is not the end: confirm the grower's record is complete, their documents are uploaded, and they know what happens next. The agent who follows through turns enrollment into outcomes, and outcomes into the program's renewal.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Field Communication and Community Trust',
                'summary' => 'Earn the trust that makes the work possible.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Field work runs on trust. Growers share their names, farms, and documents with you because they believe you represent something legitimate and honest. Building that trust, and keeping it, is a core certified-agent skill.\n\nIntroduce yourself properly. Show your agent identification, explain who you represent, and state the purpose of the visit before asking anything. A grower who knows why you are there cooperates; one who wonders whether you are a scammer does not.\n\nCommunicate in the community's language, literally and figuratively. Speak the local language where you can, and avoid platform jargon that means nothing to a grower. 'Verify your farm so the cooperative can trust it' lands better than 'capture GPS evidence for the verification workflow'.\n\nRespect the community's time and culture. Arrange visits in advance where possible, be punctual, and follow local courtesies. Ask consent before photographing people, and never accept gifts or payments; the agent who can be bought cannot be trusted with the registry.\n\nFinally, be honest about what you cannot do. If a problem is beyond your mandate, say so and connect the grower to the right channel. Trust is built by truthfulness, including the truthfulness that says 'not me, but here is who can help'.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Certification Assessment and Career Path',
                'summary' => 'Pass the assessment and grow your field career.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Certification is the beginning of your field career, not the end of training. Understanding the assessment, and the path beyond it, keeps you growing long after you pass.\n\nThe assessment tests what this program taught: registration accuracy, data standards, GPS and photo evidence, program support, and professional conduct. It is open-book in the sense that the standards are written down; the real test is applying them in the field. Study the standards, then study your own visit records against them.\n\nAfter certification, expect a supervised period. Your early visits may be sampled more heavily while the platform confirms your evidence meets the standard unaided. Treat the sampling as coaching, not suspicion; it is how the platform protects its verification quality.\n\nGrow your career deliberately. Senior agents, state coordinators, and specialist roles all come from the certified field pool. Ask for feedback on your records, volunteer for the harder assignments, and mentor new agents when you can. Teaching others is the fastest way to master the standards yourself.\n\nFinally, keep learning. The platform, the programs, and the standards evolve; the agent who stays current stays employable. Your certification is a licence to serve, and the career it builds is built one accurate record at a time.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Registration Field Guide', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Identity, contact, location, and farm facts.'],
            ['title' => 'Data Standards Quick Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Names, hierarchy, GPS, quantities, and notes.'],
            ['title' => 'Evidence Capture Checklist', 'type' => 'checklist', 'notes' => 'GPS fix, landmark photo, standard photo set, captions.'],
            ['title' => 'Program Enrollment Guide', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Eligibility, lists, honest explanation, follow-up.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Field Agent Certification Program Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What is the boundary of a field agent\'s mandate?', 'a' => 'Approve applications and promise outcomes', 'b' => 'Collect, verify, and report; approvals belong to the verification team', 'c' => 'Handle grower payments in the field', 'd' => 'Change grower records whenever asked', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why must state and LGA come from the official dropdowns?', 'a' => 'Reporting aggregates on those exact values', 'b' => 'The dropdowns are faster to type', 'c' => 'Free text is banned by law', 'd' => 'It makes the form look uniform', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'What makes a GPS coordinate defensible?', 'a' => 'Any coordinate near the farm road', 'b' => 'A stable fix at the farm\'s identifiable point, tied to a landmark photo', 'c' => 'A coordinate from a map app at home', 'd' => 'The coordinate of the nearest town', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'How should an agent build a program\'s eligible list?', 'a' => 'From friends and influential growers', 'b' => 'From the registry data matched to the program rules', 'c' => 'From whoever arrives first at the office', 'd' => 'From last year\'s list unchanged', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What should an agent do when offered a gift for enrollment help?', 'a' => 'Accept a small token politely', 'b' => 'Decline and report it; an agent who can be bought cannot be trusted', 'c' => 'Accept but record it later', 'd' => 'Charge a fee instead', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    21 => [
        'overview' => 'Marketplace Seller Certification Program certifies sellers to source quality products, craft professional listings, price for margin, fulfil reliably, and build a strong reputation. It is the complete program for sellers who treat the marketplace as a business.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Seller Certification and Marketplace Standards',
                'summary' => 'The standards every certified seller meets.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Seller certification is the marketplace's promise that a seller is verified, reliable, and honest. The certification has standards, and understanding them is the first step to meeting them.\n\nThe standards rest on four pillars. Accuracy: listings that describe the product, grade, and quantity truthfully. Reliability: orders fulfilled on time and as described. Conduct: real prices, platform payments, and professional communication. Accountability: a reachable seller who owns problems and resolves them fairly.\n\nCertification is earned through evidence: a verified business or farm identity, a track record of completed orders, and a reliability score that reflects real transactions. The badge appears when the criteria are met; it is not applied for directly, which is why every order matters.\n\nUnderstand what certification buys you. Certified sellers rank higher in buyer search, convert better, and are trusted with larger orders. Buyers pay a premium for certainty, and the badge is the marketplace's certainty signal.\n\nFinally, treat certification as a status to defend. Reliability scores update with every order, and a pattern of cancellations or disputes will cost the badge. The certified seller is the one who keeps the standards daily, not the one who earned them once.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Product Sourcing and Quality Control',
                'summary' => 'Source products you can stand behind.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Every successful marketplace seller starts at the source. Sourcing is where quality is won or lost, and quality control is the discipline that keeps it consistent from first order to hundredth.\n\nSource from verified origins. For fresh produce, that means farms and collection points you know, with a clear grade and harvest date. For processed goods, it means registered producers with compliant labels. Your sourcing is your product's provenance, and provenance is what buyers increasingly pay for.\n\nGrade ruthlessly at intake. Sort product into grades at collection, and list each grade separately with its own title and price. Mixed grades sold as one listing generate disputes and damage the reliability score; separate grades generate trust and better margins.\n\nControl quality through handling. Fresh produce is handled gently, kept cool and dry, and sold within its window; processed goods are checked for intact packaging and valid dates. A quality check before dispatch, with photos for high-value orders, prevents most disputes before they start.\n\nFinally, build sourcing relationships. A reliable supplier, a fair price, and a first-refusal agreement on the best grade are worth more than a bargain hunt. The seller with a secured supply of a consistent grade is the seller who can scale without failing customers.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Professional Listing Craft',
                'summary' => 'Listings that are findable, precise, and credible.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A listing is a sales document with a search engine behind it. Professional listing craft is the skill of making it findable by the right buyers and credible to all of them.\n\nStructure for search first. The category must be right, because buyers filter by it. The title must name the product, its grade or form, and the unit, in the vocabulary buyers actually use. Search for your own product and see what terms buyers use, then mirror them honestly.\n\nMake the description do real work. State the grade, the form, the package or bulk arrangement, the origin, and the condition honestly, including known defects or seasonal variation. A description that anticipates the buyer's questions converts better and generates fewer disputes.\n\nUse photos where possible, taken at your own farm or depot. Genuine photos with good light beat stock images every time, and buyers can tell the difference. If photos are not possible, a precise, honest description carries the weight.\n\nFinally, keep listings current. Update prices when the market moves, mark stock out the moment it sells, and refresh the description when the harvest changes the grade. A current listing is a credible listing; a stale one is a cancelled order waiting to happen.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Pricing Strategy and Margin Management',
                'summary' => 'Price to win orders and keep profit.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Pricing is where sellers win or lose the sale, and margin is where they win or lose the business. Strategy is pricing deliberately, with full knowledge of cost, market, and buyer.\n\nKnow your true cost per unit: product, harvesting or sourcing, sorting, packaging, transport, and marketplace fees, plus the cost of waste. Your floor price is true cost plus the minimum margin you need; selling below it is a loss, however busy the shop looks.\n\nRead the market before you price. Check comparable listings for the same grade and form, and position deliberately: premium pricing for a verifiable premium grade, competitive pricing for volume. Never bait with a low price you intend to negotiate upward; the marketplace penalises it and buyers abandon it.\n\nManage margin at the point of sale. Confirm quantity, grade, delivery terms, and price in writing, and quote loading, transport, and payment fees explicitly. Hidden costs are the silent margin killer that turns a profitable order into a loss.\n\nFinally, review margins monthly by product. Let the data show which listings are profit drivers and which are time-wasters, then push the first and drop the second. The seller who manages margin deliberately builds a business that survives price swings.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Fulfilment Excellence and Delivery',
                'summary' => 'Deliver on the promise, every order.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Fulfilment is the moment the listing becomes reality. Excellence means the buyer receives the agreed product, grade, and quantity, on time, in good condition, every single order.\n\nFulfil from a written order. Confirm the product, grade, quantity, price, and delivery terms before picking anything, and photograph high-value consignments at dispatch. The dispatch photo settles most quantity disputes in seconds.\n\nMaster the logistics. Know your lead times by distance and quote them honestly; time the harvest to the order for fresh produce so the buyer gets the freshest product; use clean, dry vehicles and avoid overloading that crushes and cracks. Weigh and count at dispatch and record the figures.\n\nHandle the exceptions with ownership. If a shortfall, damage, or delay happens, tell the buyer immediately with the cause and a remedy: replacement, partial refund, or a discount on the next order. Early ownership defuses complaints; silence escalates them.\n\nFinally, measure fulfilment. Track on-time rate, damage rate, and dispute rate by product and by route. The numbers tell you whether the problem is the product, the packaging, or the carrier, and they are the same numbers that drive your marketplace ranking.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Ratings, Reviews and Seller Reputation',
                'summary' => 'Build the reputation that compounds.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Reputation is the marketplace's memory of your reliability, and it compounds: every good order makes the next one easier, and every bad one makes the next one harder. Managing ratings and reviews is a deliberate practice, not luck.\n\nEarn the rating before you ask for it. Buyers rate the whole experience: the listing's accuracy, the delivery, the communication, and the product. The seller who is precise, punctual, and communicative earns the five stars without asking.\n\nAsk for feedback at the right moment. After delivery is confirmed, a polite request for a review is appropriate; before delivery, or in exchange for anything, is not. Genuine, unsolicited reviews are the only ones that build trust, and buyers can tell the difference.\n\nHandle negative reviews as data. A critical review is a free audit of your operation. Respond professionally, fix the cause if it is real, and let the improvement show in later orders. Buyers read how you respond more closely than the complaint itself.\n\nFinally, watch the trend, not the single score. One bad review among many is noise; a falling trend is a signal. The seller who reads the trend and acts on it keeps a reputation that outlasts any season, price war, or competitor.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Scaling a Marketplace Business',
                'summary' => 'Grow capacity without breaking quality.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Scaling is the art of growing volume while holding the standards that earned your reputation. The marketplace rewards growth that preserves quality and punishes growth that breaks it.\n\nScale in steps, not leaps. Add capacity, products, or coverage only when the current operation is reliably excellent, and only after the process is documented and trainable. Growth that outruns your team's ability to deliver shows up as cancellations, which the marketplace algorithm notices immediately.\n\nStandardise before you expand. The listing method, the quality check, the fulfilment steps, and the communication style should be written down so a new team member or a new region repeats them exactly. The seller who scales on written standards scales invisibly to buyers.\n\nDiversify intelligently. New grades, new forms of the same product, or adjacent buyer segments you already serve well are lower-risk growth than a leap into the unknown. Let your sales data show which direction the demand already points.\n\nFinally, protect the core. Your rating, your reliability score, and your review trend are the assets that generate every future sale. Any expansion that threatens them is too fast. The seller who grows at the speed of their quality builds a marketplace business that lasts.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Seller Standards Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Accuracy, reliability, conduct, accountability pillars.'],
            ['title' => 'Sourcing and QC Checklist', 'type' => 'checklist', 'notes' => 'Verified origins, grading, handling, and dispatch photos.'],
            ['title' => 'Listing Craft Worksheet', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Category, title, description, photos, and freshness.'],
            ['title' => 'Fulfilment Metrics Tracker', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'On-time, damage, and dispute rates by product and route.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Marketplace Seller Certification Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What are the four pillars of seller standards?', 'a' => 'Price, speed, stock, and photos', 'b' => 'Accuracy, reliability, conduct, and accountability', 'c' => 'Marketing, shipping, returns, and taxes', 'd' => 'Friends, reviews, ads, and discounts', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why grade products at intake and list grades separately?', 'a' => 'Mixed-grade listings generate disputes; separate grades build trust and margin', 'b' => 'Grading is required by law for all products', 'c' => 'It makes the catalog longer', 'd' => 'Buyers cannot choose otherwise', 'correct' => 'A'],
                ['question' => 'What is the correct response to a delivery shortfall?', 'a' => 'Wait to see if the buyer notices', 'b' => 'Tell the buyer immediately with the cause and a remedy', 'c' => 'Offer a discount only if the buyer complains', 'd' => 'Cancel the order and re-list the product', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'How should a seller treat a negative review?', 'a' => 'Argue with the buyer publicly', 'b' => 'Respond professionally and fix the real cause', 'c' => 'Delete the listing it came from', 'd' => 'Ignore it; reviews do not matter', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the safest growth strategy for a marketplace seller?', 'a' => 'Expand as fast as possible to capture demand', 'b' => 'Scale in steps that preserve quality and standards', 'c' => 'Add products before testing the first one', 'd' => 'Cut prices below cost to win volume', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    134 => [
        'overview' => 'State Coordinator Operations Program is the flagship leadership program for state coordinators. It covers state operations leadership, registry growth and quality, field force development, program management, data-driven management, stakeholder engagement, and state business planning. The most comprehensive program in the Academy.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'State Operations Leadership',
                'summary' => 'Lead the state operation with clarity and rhythm.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A state coordinator is a leader, not just a manager of tasks. Leadership in operations means setting direction, building the team, and running a rhythm that keeps the state moving whether or not you are present.\n\nStart with direction. Write down the state's priorities for the quarter: coverage targets, verification quality, program delivery, and data quality. A team without written priorities invents its own, and invented priorities rarely match the platform's strategy.\n\nBuild the team deliberately. Know each agent's strengths, assign work that fits, and develop the gaps with coaching rather than blame. The coordinator who develops two strong senior agents has doubled their own reach; the one who does everything themselves bottlenecks the state.\n\nRun the rhythm. Weekly, review agent activity and evidence quality; monthly, review the state's numbers against targets; quarterly, review strategy with the national team. The rhythm is what turns a list of priorities into progress, and it survives your holidays and your travel days.\n\nFinally, lead by example on integrity. The coordinator who follows the evidence standards, documents decisions, and escalates problems honestly sets the tone for the whole state. Culture in an operation is simply what the leader repeats.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Registry Growth and Quality at State Scale',
                'summary' => 'Grow the registry without letting quality slip.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The registry is the platform's foundation in your state, and your job is to grow it fast without letting quality slip. Growth without quality is worse than no growth, because bad records poison every program that uses them.\n\nMeasure growth honestly. Track new verified records, not new signups, and split the metric by LGA to see where growth is real and where it is stalled. A state that adds unverified signups is inflating a number that no program can use.\n\nDrive growth through coverage. Unregistered growers hide in the LGAs you barely reach, so the growth plan is a coverage plan: which LGAs, which agents, which weeks. Pair every registration drive with a verification capacity plan, because registrations without verification are just promises.\n\nGuard quality at the source. Registration standards, dropdown discipline, and same-day submission are the controls that keep the registry clean. Sample registrations weekly the way you sample verifications, because a bad record that enters the registry is a bad record every program inherits.\n\nFinally, clean the backlog. Duplicates, unverified phones, and stale records accumulate in every growing registry. A monthly data-quality pass, with clear owners per LGA, keeps the state's foundation solid under the growth.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Field Force Leadership and Development',
                'summary' => 'Develop agents into a high-performing field force.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Your field force is the state's engine, and field force leadership is the discipline of developing agents into people who perform without you watching. Development is the multiplier on every other result you achieve.\n\nHire and assign well. Recruit for reliability and integrity, then assign agents to the LGAs where they are most effective, considering coverage gaps, language, and travel. A well-assigned agent in a gap LGA outperforms a brilliant agent in a saturated one.\n\nTrain to the standards. Every agent should know the registration, data, and evidence standards cold. Train with the written standards and a supervised first visit; then sample their work weekly against the same standards. Coaching that points at the standard, the gap, and the fix works; criticism that points at the person does not.\n\nMotivate with clarity and recognition. Agents who know their targets, see their progress, and get recognised for quality work stay and improve. Publish the best evidence examples, not just the numbers, because recognition of quality drives more quality.\n\nFinally, plan for retention and succession. Losing a good agent costs months of productivity, so develop senior agents who can cover, train, and eventually lead. The field force that grows its own leaders is the field force that never stalls.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Program Management for State Coordinators',
                'summary' => 'Deliver national programs to state outcomes.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Programs are how the platform delivers value, and the state coordinator is the program manager for their state. Program management is the discipline of turning a national plan into state outcomes, on time and honestly reported.\n\nStart with the program brief: the target audience, the eligibility rules, the benefit, the timeline, and the success measures. Translate the brief into a state plan: the eligible list from the registry, the agents assigned, the communication to growers, and the weekly delivery targets.\n\nExecute with monitoring. Track reach against the eligible list daily during the campaign: contacted, enrolled, and served. The gap between plan and reality is your early warning, and it is actionable only while the campaign is still live.\n\nManage the risks. Programs fail at the edges: ineligible enrollments, duplicate claims, and communication gaps in remote LGAs. Mitigate them with clear rules for agents, sample checks, and targeted outreach to the hardest-to-reach areas.\n\nFinally, report outcomes honestly. Program evaluations use your numbers, and inflated reporting poisons the next program's design and the state's credibility. Record what actually happened, including shortfalls and causes. The coordinator who delivers and reports honestly earns the right to compete for more programs.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Data-Driven State Management',
                'summary' => 'Run the state on evidence, not on anecdotes.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The state is too large to manage by walking around alone. Data-driven management is the discipline of using dashboards, sampling, and trends to see the whole state and act on what matters.\n\nLearn your dashboards until the definitions are second nature: verified records, coverage, verification quality, agent activity, and program reach. A misunderstood metric leads to a wrong conclusion, and a wrong conclusion leads to a wrong decision for the whole state.\n\nRead trends, not single weeks. Four weeks of decline is a signal; one slow week is noise. Compare your state to its own history and to peer states, and ask what changed when the trend turned: a new agent, a seasonal effect, a program launch, or a data defect.\n\nDrill before you act. A spike in rejections, a cluster of failed verifications, a drop in one LGA: each has a cause that the data points to and the agents explain. Investigate before concluding, because the dashboard shows the symptom and the field holds the diagnosis.\n\nFinally, run the state's data quality. Your dashboards are only as honest as the records behind them. A monthly pass on duplicates, unverified contacts, and stale records keeps the intelligence clean, and clean intelligence is the coordinator's advantage over every problem the state throws up.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Stakeholder Engagement and Advocacy',
                'summary' => 'Represent the state to the platform and the field.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "The state coordinator sits between two worlds: the national platform and the state's growers, agents, and providers. Stakeholder engagement is the discipline of serving both, honestly, and advocating for the state without inflating its numbers.\n\nEngage the national team as a partner. Share your state's data, your blockers, and your needs early, not only at review time. A coordinator who asks for help in April gets resources in May; the one who waits for the annual review gets them next year.\n\nEngage growers where they are. Field days, cooperative meetings, and market visits build the trust that drives registration, verification, and program enrollment. The platform's best-designed program fails in a state whose growers do not know it exists.\n\nAdvocate with evidence. When the state needs more agents, a program, or a policy exception, make the case with data: the coverage gap, the eligible growers, the expected outcome. Evidence-based advocacy wins resources; volume-based advocacy wins eye-rolls.\n\nFinally, manage expectations in both directions. Do not promise growers outcomes the platform has not approved, and do not promise the national team results your field force cannot deliver. The coordinator who is trusted in both directions is the one whose state gets the resources and the programs.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'State Business Planning and Review',
                'summary' => 'Plan the state like a business, review like an owner.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The best coordinators run their state like a business: a written plan, clear targets, disciplined execution, and honest review. Business planning is the discipline that turns a year of activity into a year of results.\n\nWrite the annual state plan. Coverage targets by LGA, registry growth, verification quality, program delivery, and team development, each with a number and an owner. The plan is the yardstick that every monthly review measures against.\n\nBudget your scarcest resource: field hours. Agent time is finite, and the plan should allocate it to the highest-value work, which is usually the weakest coverage and the programs with the largest eligible lists. A plan that does not allocate time is a wish list.\n\nReview monthly and quarterly. Monthly, compare results to plan and investigate the gaps; quarterly, review the strategy itself: are the priorities still right, what did the data teach you, what changes next quarter. The quarterly strategy review is where the year is actually steered.\n\nFinally, close the year with an honest retrospective. What worked, what failed, and what you will change. The coordinator who runs this cycle, plan-do-review-improve, builds a state operation that improves every year, regardless of who holds the role.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'State Operations Plan Template', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Priorities, targets, owners, and field-hour allocation.'],
            ['title' => 'Registry Growth Dashboard Guide', 'type' => 'guide', 'notes' => 'Verified growth, coverage, and quality metrics.'],
            ['title' => 'Agent Development Plan', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Strengths, assignments, coaching, and succession.'],
            ['title' => 'Stakeholder Engagement Map', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'National team, growers, providers, and advocacy cases.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'State Coordinator Operations Program Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What turns a list of priorities into progress?', 'a' => 'A written annual report', 'b' => 'A review rhythm: weekly, monthly, and quarterly', 'c' => 'More meetings with the national team', 'd' => 'Higher targets for the agents', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why is registry growth measured by verified records, not signups?', 'a' => 'Signups are harder to count', 'b' => 'Unverified signups are numbers no program can use', 'c' => 'The platform charges per signup', 'd' => 'Signups are private data', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the fastest way to double a coordinator\'s reach?', 'a' => 'Work longer hours personally', 'b' => 'Develop strong senior agents who can lead and train', 'c' => 'Hire twice as many agents at once', 'd' => 'Merge two states together', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'How should a coordinator advocate for state resources?', 'a' => 'By repeating the request loudly and often', 'b' => 'With evidence: the coverage gap, eligible growers, and expected outcome', 'c' => 'By promising inflated results', 'd' => 'Only during the annual review', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the purpose of a quarterly strategy review?', 'a' => 'To rewrite the entire plan from scratch', 'b' => 'To check the priorities are still right and steer the year', 'c' => 'To celebrate the year\'s completion', 'd' => 'To replace underperforming agents', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    149 => [
        'overview' => 'Coconut Nursery and Seedling Quality trains nursery operators to run nurseries that produce premium seedlings. It covers site and infrastructure, seed nut sourcing and quality, germination technique, seedling quality standards, pest and disease management, packaging and transport, and nursery records and assurance.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Nursery Site and Infrastructure',
                'summary' => 'Choose and build a site that grows uniform seedlings.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A nursery's quality begins with its site. The right location, layout, and infrastructure make uniform seedlings possible; the wrong site fights you every week with water, heat, and access problems.\n\nChoose the site for water, drainage, and sun. You need a reliable water source, good drainage so beds never waterlog, and open sky for the germination beds, with shade available for the young seedlings. Avoid low-lying ground where rain pools, and avoid deep shade that stretches germination.\n\nLayout for workflow and biosecurity. Arrange the nursery so seed nuts arrive, are cured and float-tested, sown, raised, hardened, and dispatched in a logical flow. Keep the quarantine area separate, because a diseased batch should never sit beside your best seedlings.\n\nBuild the basics well: raised or level beds according to drainage, a water storage and watering system, shade nets or structures at fifty to sixty percent, and secure fencing against animals. A fenced nursery loses fewer seedlings than an open one, and the loss is silent until the count.\n\nFinally, plan for the record. A nursery logbook, a batch board, and a storage area for labels and tools are infrastructure too. The operator who can find last season's germination records builds better than the one who remembers.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Seed Nut Sourcing and Quality',
                'summary' => 'The seed nut decides the seedling.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Seedling quality is capped by seed nut quality. Sourcing and selecting seed nuts well is the single highest-leverage decision a nursery operator makes, and it deserves a rigorous process.\n\nSource from proven parent palms. Select palms that are healthy, regular-bearing, and high-yielding, ideally from a register of elite or certified palms. Documented parentage is the first quality signal a buyer asks for, and it is the difference between a masterclass nursery and an ordinary one.\n\nHarvest at full maturity. Mature nuts have a thick brown husk, a dense shell that does not rattle, and a full kernel. Immature nuts germinate poorly and grow weak seedlings. After harvest, cure the nuts for a few weeks in a shaded, ventilated area; curing reduces rot and evens out germination.\n\nSelect before you sow. Float-test the nuts and discard floaters, which usually have hollow or damaged kernels. Grade the sinkers by size so the bed is uniform, and record the source palm, harvest date, and grade for every batch. That record is what lets you trace any batch that underperforms.\n\nFinally, guard against the classic traps: nuts harvested after a long drought, nuts stored in damp conditions, and nuts from palms with unknown history. The operator who buys cheap, unproven nuts saves money once and loses a season of reputation.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Germination and Nursery Techniques',
                'summary' => 'Techniques that produce even, strong germination.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Germination technique turns good seed nuts into uniform seedlings. The variables are few, but they must be controlled: media, moisture, spacing, and shade.\n\nPrepare the media right. A coarse sand and well-decomposed organic mix drains freely; heavy clay holds water and rots nuts. Raise beds where drainage is poor, and level the surface so water does not pool at one end. The media is the seed nut's first home, and it should feel like a damp sponge, not a swamp.\n\nSow with care. Place nuts on their sides, half-buried, with the eyes pointing sideways so the shoot emerges naturally. Spacing of roughly two nut-widths lets each seedling grow evenly and makes transplanting safe. Mark rows so every batch can be counted and tracked.\n\nWater and shade to keep conditions uniform. Keep the media moist but never waterlogged, adjusting for rain and heat. Young seedlings scorch in direct sun, so maintain fifty to sixty percent shade until they have several leaves, then harden them off gradually before field planting.\n\nFinally, track germination by batch: sowing date, first germination, and full germination counts. A batch that germinates slowly or unevenly tells you something about the source or the curing, and the record is the only way to know which. Uniformity is the product; the technique is how you sell it.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Seedling Quality Standards',
                'summary' => 'What a premium seedling looks like, and how to grade.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Buyers judge a nursery by its seedlings, so quality must be defined, visible, and enforced. Seedling quality standards turn 'good seedlings' from an opinion into a grade you can sell.\n\nDefine the premium seedling: a healthy, undamaged nut with a firm kernel, a strong shoot of the expected height for its age, a robust root system, and uniform size within the batch. Any seedling with a cracked nut, a damaged shoot, or signs of rot is culled, not sold.\n\nGrade and sort at transplanting and again at dispatch. Grading at two points catches the weaklings that hide among the strong, and it lets you price honestly by grade. A batch sold as premium that contains ten percent culls is a batch that returns to your reputation as complaints.\n\nAge and harden seedlings properly. Seedlings are sold by age as much as size, and a hardened seedling, gradually acclimated to sun and field conditions, survives transplanting far better than a soft nursery plant. State the age and hardening status on every sale.\n\nFinally, inspect before dispatch. Count, grade, and check every batch against the standard, and reject anything that fails. The nursery whose dispatch inspection is non-negotiable is the nursery whose name buyers trust at the next planting season.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Pest and Disease Management in Nurseries',
                'summary' => 'Protect the nursery before problems spread.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Nurseries concentrate plants, and concentration invites pests and diseases. Nursery protection is the discipline of prevention first, detection early, and control precise.\n\nPrevent through hygiene and design. Remove fallen nuts and decaying organic matter, which harbour rhinoceros beetle grubs; keep beds weeded; separate batches so a problem in one cannot spread to all; and keep the nursery fenced against animals that dig and carry disease. Prevention is cheaper than any treatment.\n\nDetect by walking the nursery. A daily walk, eyes on the media, the nuts, and the shoots, catches problems when they are small. Look for bore holes and V-shaped cuts in shoots, soft or discoloured nut tissue, and unusual wilting. Early detection is the difference between losing a batch and losing a season.\n\nControl precisely and safely. Remove and destroy affected material first; use a chemical only if the label covers the pest and the crop, at the labelled rate, with the labelled PPE, and away from water sources. Never spray a nursery on suspicion; a wrong chemical kills beneficial organisms and wastes money.\n\nFinally, record every sighting and treatment. The nursery log of pests and controls becomes your early-warning system: the same pest returning each season, the batch that always rots. Records turn a reactive nursery into a resilient one.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Packaging, Transport and Sale of Seedlings',
                'summary' => 'Deliver seedlings that arrive as premium.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A premium seedling that arrives damaged is a lost sale and a lost customer. Packaging, transport, and sale discipline is what protects your quality through the last mile.\n\nPackage for the seedling's weakness. Coconut seedlings are vulnerable to desiccation and physical damage, so pack them to keep roots moist and shaded, with the nut and shoot protected from crushing. Use ventilated containers, and never pack so tightly that leaves and shoots rub or break.\n\nTransport with care. Shade the load from direct sun, avoid overnight exposure to cold or rain, and drive gently; vibration and tipping damage roots and snap shoots. For long distances, schedule dispatch so the seedlings are planted soon after arrival, and tell the buyer the best planting window.\n\nSell with the grade visible. Each batch should be labelled with variety, age, grade, and batch number, so the buyer knows exactly what they are buying and you can trace any complaint back to the bed. A labelled batch is a professional sale; an unlabelled sack is a market stall.\n\nFinally, follow up after sale. Ask how the seedlings took after planting; the answer is your best quality data. The nursery that learns from every delivery improves its hardening, packaging, and timing, and its reputation compounds with every planting season.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Nursery Records and Quality Assurance',
                'summary' => 'The records that make quality provable.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Quality assurance in a nursery is not a certificate on the wall; it is a set of records that proves, batch by batch, that your process is controlled. Buyers, certification programs, and your own improvement all depend on those records.\n\nKeep the batch record: source palms, harvest and cure dates, float-test results, sowing date, germination counts, and any treatments. The batch record lets you trace any batch forward to its buyers and backward to its origin, which is the core of traceability.\n\nKeep the nursery log: daily conditions, watering, pest sightings, weeding, and incidents. The log reveals patterns, such as a bed that always rots or a pest that returns each month, and patterns are where improvement lives.\n\nKeep the sales record: which batch went to which buyer, in what grade and quantity. Combined with the batch record, this is your recall capability, and it is what turns a complaint into a fixable event rather than a scandal.\n\nFinally, review the records monthly and seasonally. Compare germination rates across batches and seasons, cull rates across beds, and customer feedback across years. The nursery whose records are read, not just written, is the nursery whose quality rises every season.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Nursery Site Planning Sheet', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Water, drainage, layout, shade, and fencing.'],
            ['title' => 'Seed Nut Selection Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Parent palms, maturity, float test, grading.'],
            ['title' => 'Seedling Grading Standard', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Premium seedling definition and culling rules.'],
            ['title' => 'Nursery Batch Record', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Source, harvest, sowing, germination, treatments, sales.'],
        ],
        'assessment' => [
            'title' => 'Coconut Nursery and Seedling Quality Assessment',
            'instructions' => 'Answer all questions. A score of 70% or higher is required to pass. You have up to 3 attempts.',
            'pass_score' => 70,
            'questions' => [
                ['question' => 'What is the highest-leverage decision a nursery operator makes?', 'a' => 'The price of the seedlings', 'b' => 'Sourcing and selecting quality seed nuts', 'c' => 'The size of the nursery sign', 'd' => 'The number of workers hired', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why float-test seed nuts before sowing?', 'a' => 'To wash the nuts before planting', 'b' => 'Floaters usually have hollow or damaged kernels', 'c' => 'To sort nuts by colour', 'd' => 'It is a traditional ritual with no effect', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What shade level suits young coconut seedlings?', 'a' => 'Full direct sun all day', 'b' => 'Fifty to sixty percent, then gradual hardening', 'c' => 'Total shade until transplanting', 'd' => 'Shade is only needed in winter', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'What is the first control for a nursery pest outbreak?', 'a' => 'Spray the whole nursery immediately', 'b' => 'Remove and destroy affected material, then treat precisely if needed', 'c' => 'Close the nursery and wait', 'd' => 'Water the beds more heavily', 'correct' => 'B'],
                ['question' => 'Why label each batch with variety, age, grade, and batch number?', 'a' => 'Labels make the sacks look professional', 'b' => 'It enables traceability and turns complaints into fixable events', 'c' => 'Buyers are required to photograph labels', 'd' => 'It is only needed for export sales', 'correct' => 'B'],
            ],
        ],
    ],

    150 => [
        'overview' => 'Coconut Pest, Disease and Farm Health Monitoring trains growers and field officers to run systematic farm health monitoring. It covers monitoring foundations, major pests and diseases, scouting methods, safe and sustainable control, treatment records, and building a farm health plan.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Farm Health Monitoring Foundations',
                'summary' => 'Why monitoring beats calendar spraying.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Farm health monitoring is the disciplined observation of your palms, regularly and systematically, so problems are caught while they are small. It is the foundation of every effective pest and disease program, and it beats calendar spraying for three reasons.\n\nFirst, monitoring tells you what is actually present. Spraying on a calendar treats problems that may not exist and misses the ones that do. Scouting tells you which pest, in which block, at what level, so the control matches the reality.\n\nSecond, monitoring catches problems early. A pest population, a disease patch, or a nutrient deficiency is cheapest to control when it is small and local. A weekly walk finds a beetle attack in one block; a quarterly inspection finds it across the farm.\n\nThird, monitoring builds knowledge. Your records of what appeared, when, and what worked become your farm's health history. Over seasons, that history predicts the outbreaks, so you are preparing rather than reacting.\n\nSet the routine: a defined route, a defined frequency, and a defined record. Walk the same route each time so nothing is skipped, note the weather, and record what you see per block. Ten minutes a week on a route is the cheapest insurance a coconut farm can buy.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Major Coconut Pests and Their Management',
                'summary' => 'Know the key pests and the right response to each.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A handful of pests cause most coconut damage, and knowing each one's signs and cycle is the core of farm health. Learn these, and you can diagnose most field visits.\n\nThe rhinoceros beetle attacks the crown, leaving characteristic bore holes and V-shaped cuts in the spears and fronds. Its grubs develop in decaying organic matter, so the cultural defence is a clean crown and managed compost. Remove and destroy badly damaged spears; severe attacks on young palms may justify targeted treatment.\n\nLeaf-eating caterpillars strip fronds in patches. Their presence is visible as chewed edges and webbing; light infestations can be managed by removing affected fronds, and natural predators keep them in check when chemicals are used sparingly. Broad spraying kills those predators and makes the problem worse.\n\nScale insects appear as immobile bumps on fronds, nuts, and stems, often with sooty mould feeding on their honeydew. They spread by crawling and on tools; clean tools between trees and manage ants that farm them. Control is targeted, not blanket.\n\nFinally, remember that a healthy, well-nourished palm tolerates pests far better than a stressed one. Pest management begins with farm health: water, nutrition, and sanitation. The grower who keeps palms strong fights fewer pests.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Major Coconut Diseases and Their Management',
                'summary' => 'Recognise the serious diseases and stop their spread.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Diseases are harder to reverse than pests, because most palm diseases have no cure. Recognition, sanitation, and prevention are the management tools, and speed matters.\n\nBud rot attacks the emerging spear, which blackens and collapses, often after wet weather or crown injury. It can kill young palms. Manage it by keeping the crown clean and dry, removing affected tissue promptly, and avoiding injury to the spear during harvest and pruning.\n\nLeaf rot shows as streaked, rotting fronds, usually in humid, crowded canopies. Improve air flow by removing dead and hanging fronds, and avoid wetting the canopy when watering or applying products. Sanitation removes the inoculum that keeps the disease cycling.\n\nStem bleeding weeps dark fluid from cracks in the trunk, often after mechanical injury or storm damage. There is no chemical cure; protect trunks from damage, and isolate badly affected palms to reduce spread to neighbours.\n\nFinally, quarantine and report. A suspicious, rapidly spreading, or unknown disease pattern should be photographed, isolated where practical, and reported to the cooperative or a plant health authority the same day. Early reporting is the farm's, and the region's, best defence.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Scouting Methods and Monitoring Routines',
                'summary' => 'A scouting routine that finds problems early.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Scouting is a skill with a method. A good routine is systematic, frequent, and recorded, and it turns a walk around the farm into an early-warning system.\n\nPlan the route. Cover every block, starting from the entrance and working a fixed pattern, so no area is skipped. Pay attention to the edges, the low spots, and the blocks near roads or other farms, where pests often arrive first.\n\nLook at three levels: the crown (spears, fronds, holes, cuts), the trunk (cracks, bleeding, lesions), and the ground (fallen nuts, weeds, decaying matter, grub activity). Many pests and diseases show at one level more than the others, so check all three.\n\nSet the frequency. Weekly in the normal season, and twice weekly during the risky periods: after heavy rain, during dry stress, or when neighbouring farms report problems. Frequency should follow risk, not the calendar.\n\nRecord as you go: date, block, weather, what you saw, and how much. A photo with the block and date in the note is worth a paragraph. The routine that records builds the farm's health history, and the history is what turns scouting from a walk into intelligence.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Safe and Sustainable Control',
                'summary' => 'Control pests with the least harm to farm and environment.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Sustainable control follows a clear order: cultural methods first, biological second, chemical last, and always at the labelled rate. The order protects the farm, the worker, and the consumer.\n\nCultural controls come first because they are free and permanent. Clean the crown of dead fronds where grubs breed, remove and destroy diseased tissue, keep the farm weeded, and manage compost and decaying matter away from palms. These steps remove the pest's home and food.\n\nBiological controls come second. Many pests have natural predators, parasites, or pathogens that keep them in check when we do not kill the predators with broad sprays. Preserve beneficial insects, and use targeted products that spare them. A farm with a healthy predator population needs fewer treatments.\n\nChemical controls are the last resort, and when used, they are used precisely: the right product for the right pest, at the labelled rate, in the right weather, with the right PPE, and with the pre-harvest interval observed so residues never reach the nut. Never mix products or increase rates; both are unsafe and ineffective.\n\nFinally, evaluate after every control. Did the pest level fall? Did the damage stop? The evaluation, recorded, is what makes the next control smarter. Sustainable control is a cycle of observe, act, and learn.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Treatment Records and Compliance',
                'summary' => 'Record every treatment; prove every claim.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "Treatment records are the evidence that your farm is managed responsibly. Buyers, certification programs, and regulators increasingly ask for them, and the grower who keeps them answers in minutes while the grower who does not answers never.\n\nRecord every application: date, product name, formulation, quantity, rate, block treated, weather, and the person who applied. Keep the label and the receipt with the record. This single habit satisfies most audit and traceability requirements in one go.\n\nUnderstand why the records matter beyond compliance. They let you evaluate whether a treatment worked, by comparing treated blocks against your monitoring data. They let you trace any batch of nuts back to the treatments it received. And they protect you if a residue question or a product recall ever arises.\n\nObserve the pre-harvest interval religiously, and record it. The interval between the last application and harvest is what keeps residues out of the nuts, and the record is the proof. A buyer who asks can be shown the date; a guess is not proof.\n\nFinally, store the records simply and safely: a notebook or a spreadsheet, dated and complete, with the labels in one folder. Ten minutes a week of recording is the difference between a farm that is audit-ready and one that is not.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Building a Farm Health Plan',
                'summary' => 'Turn monitoring into a season-long health plan.',
                'duration' => 60,
                'content' => "A farm health plan pulls monitoring, knowledge, and control into one season-long strategy. It is short, practical, and reviewed every season, and it is what turns reactive spraying into managed farm health.\n\nStart from your history. Review last season's records: which pests and diseases appeared, when, and in which blocks; which treatments worked; and what the weather did. Your history predicts your risks, so the plan targets the likely problems, not all possible ones.\n\nSet the monitoring routine for the season: route, frequency, and the thresholds that trigger action. A threshold is the pest or damage level at which control is justified; below it, monitoring continues. Thresholds prevent both under-reaction and pointless spraying.\n\nSchedule the preventive work: sanitation, crown cleaning, compost management, nutrition, and the timing of any planned treatments around the risks your history predicts. Prevention scheduled is prevention done; prevention hoped for is prevention forgotten.\n\nFinally, close the loop. At season's end, compare the plan to the records: what appeared, what was done, what it cost, and what worked. The farm health plan that improves each season is the grower's quiet advantage, and it compounds into a farm that stays healthy with less effort every year.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Scouting Route Planner', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Route, frequency, and the three levels to check.'],
            ['title' => 'Pest and Disease Identification Card', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'Signs, cycles, and first responses for key pests and diseases.'],
            ['title' => 'Treatment Record Sheet', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Date, product, rate, block, weather, applicator, and PHI.'],
            ['title' => 'Farm Health Plan Template', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'History, risks, thresholds, prevention schedule, and review.'],
