    151 => [
        'overview' => 'Cooperative Governance and Member Accountability trains cooperative leaders and members to run a transparent, accountable cooperative. It covers cooperative principles and governance, structures, member records, financial transparency, decision making, conflict resolution, and audits with continuous improvement.',
        'duration' => 420,
        'lessons' => [
            [
                'title' => 'Cooperative Principles and Governance',
                'summary' => 'The principles that make a cooperative a cooperative.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A cooperative is not just a group of farmers; it is a member-owned business governed by defined principles. Understanding those principles is the foundation of governance, because they are the rules that keep the business serving its members.\n\nThe classic cooperative principles include voluntary and open membership, democratic member control, member economic participation, autonomy, education and training, cooperation among cooperatives, and concern for community. Each principle has a practical consequence: who may join, who decides, and who benefits.\n\nDemocratic control is the heart of it. Members elect the board, the board hires and oversees management, and management runs the operations. When that chain works, the cooperative serves the members; when it breaks, the cooperative serves whoever controls the chain, and the members pay the price.\n\nGovernance is the system of rules and meetings that keeps the chain honest: a constitution, clear roles, regular general assemblies, and financial reporting that members can read. Governance is not paperwork; it is the machinery of accountability.\n\nFinally, remember that every member is an owner. A cooperative whose members understand the principles, attend the assemblies, and ask questions is a cooperative that is hard to capture by a few. Governance starts with an informed membership.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Structures: Members, Board, and Management',
                'summary' => 'Who does what, and why the separation matters.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "A cooperative runs on three layers: the members who own it, the board that governs it, and the management that operates it. Clear structures, with real separation, are what keep each layer doing its own job.\n\nThe members are the owners. They join, they pay their shares, they vote at the general assembly, and they elect the board. Their powers are the ultimate ones: approving the constitution, the budget, and the auditors, and removing directors who fail them.\n\nThe board is elected to govern. It sets strategy, approves major policies, hires and supervises the manager, and protects the cooperative's assets and reputation. The board governs; it does not run the daily operations, and a board that micro-manages paralyses the manager it hired.\n\nManagement runs the operations: staff, programs, purchases, sales, and records. The manager reports to the board, and the board reports to the members. The separation is not bureaucracy; it is what makes each layer accountable to the one below it.\n\nFinally, write it down. The constitution, the by-laws, and the role descriptions make the structure real and enforceable. The cooperative with a written structure survives leadership changes; the one run by habit collapses with them.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Member Registration and Records',
                'summary' => 'A member register that is accurate and current.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "The member register is the cooperative's foundation: it defines who owns the business, who votes, and who shares in the surplus. Accurate, current member records are the first requirement of accountable governance.\n\nRegister every member properly: name, contact details, share or contribution, date of joining, and the farm or business they bring. The register should be able to answer, at any moment, who the members are, what they hold, and how to reach them.\n\nKeep the register current. Members join, leave, transfer shares, or change contact details, and each change should be recorded promptly with the supporting document. A stale register produces wrong votes, wrong dividends, and disputes that consume the general assembly.\n\nProtect the register. Member data is sensitive personal information; limit who can edit it, keep a backup, and never expose the list to non-members. The register is also a compliance document, so it should be backed up and auditable.\n\nFinally, reconcile the register against reality at least annually: count the members, verify the shares, and confirm the contact details. The cooperative whose register matches its membership is the cooperative whose decisions are legitimate and whose members trust the count.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Financial Transparency and Accountability',
                'summary' => 'Money that members can see and question.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Financial transparency is the cooperative's promise that members can see where the money comes from, where it goes, and what is left. Accountability is what happens when members can question it and get answers.\n\nReport the finances regularly and readably. A simple income-and-expense summary, a balance sheet, and a cash position, presented at each general assembly in language members understand, are the minimum. A finance report only the treasurer can read is not transparency.\n\nSeparate the money cleanly. Cooperative funds, member shares, and any program or donor funds should be accounted for separately, so each is used for its purpose and can be audited. Mixing funds is the classic path to confusion, misuse, and lost trust.\n\nControl the spending. Approvals, signatures, and documentation for every transaction, with a clear rule for who can authorise what. Dual control over cash and bank movements protects everyone, including the treasurer, from suspicion and error.\n\nFinally, distribute the surplus by rule, not by favour. The constitution should define how surpluses are shared or reinvested, and the books should show it happening. The cooperative whose money is visible, controlled, and shared by rule is the cooperative members are proud to belong to.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Decision Making and Member Voice',
                'summary' => 'Decisions that reflect the members, made openly.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Decision making is where governance becomes real. A cooperative's decisions should reflect its members, be made openly, and be recorded so anyone can see how and why they were made.\n\nUse the general assembly well. It is the supreme decision-making body: electing the board, approving budgets and audits, and deciding major policies. A well-run assembly has an agenda circulated in advance, a quorum, and minutes that record the decisions and the votes.\n\nBring the members into the decisions that affect them. Consult on the programs, the prices, and the policies that touch their farms and incomes. Consultation is not weakness; it is how the cooperative learns what members need and how it keeps its legitimacy.\n\nMake decisions by rule. The constitution defines what needs a simple majority, a two-thirds majority, or a unanimous vote, and it defines the quorum. Decisions taken outside the rules are challenges waiting to happen, and the challenge usually lands on the cooperative's weakest member.\n\nFinally, record and publish. Minutes, votes, and the reasons for major decisions should be available to members. The cooperative that decides openly builds a membership that trusts the decisions, even when their own view did not prevail.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Conflict Resolution and Discipline',
                'summary' => 'Resolve disputes fairly and protect the cooperative.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Conflicts are normal in any organisation of people, and in a cooperative they involve members who are also owners. Handling disputes and discipline fairly is a core governance skill, because mishandled conflict destroys member trust faster than any financial loss.\n\nSet the process before you need it. The constitution or by-laws should define how disputes are raised, heard, and appealed: to the manager, the board, or a disputes committee. A known process is calming; an improvised one feels like a fix.\n\nHear both sides, with evidence. Whether the dispute is over a share, a transaction, or a member's conduct, gather the facts and documents, give each party a fair hearing, and decide by the rules and the evidence. The appearance of fairness matters as much as the fairness itself.\n\nSeparate the person from the problem. Disciplinary cases should follow a defined procedure: the allegation, the evidence, the hearing, the decision, and the right to appeal. Discipline by rumour or by faction is how cooperatives split.\n\nFinally, learn from the pattern. Recurring disputes over the same issue, such as share transfers or procurement, usually signal a rule that is unclear or a process that is broken. Fix the rule, and the disputes stop. Fair conflict resolution keeps the cooperative whole.",
            ],
            [
                'title' => 'Audits, Compliance and Continuous Improvement',
                'summary' => 'Independent checks that keep the cooperative honest.',
                'duration' => 60,
                "content" => "Audits and compliance are the independent checks that prove the cooperative is run as it promises. They are not adversarial; they are the machinery that lets members trust their own organisation.\n\nAudit annually, with an independent auditor where possible. The auditor checks the books against the records, confirms the register and the shares, and reports to the members, not to the board alone. An audit the members can question is an audit that protects them.\n\nComply with the law and the constitution. Cooperatives are regulated, and the filing, reporting, and record-keeping requirements exist to protect members and the public. Compliance is a duty of the board, and the calendar of obligations should be tracked so nothing lapses.\n\nAct on the findings. An audit that finds weak controls, missing records, or unclear procedures is a gift: it shows you the fix before a loss does. Implement the recommendations, and report the implementation to the members.\n\nFinally, improve continuously. Review the governance itself annually: are the meetings well run, is the register current, do members attend and ask questions? The cooperative that audits, complies, and improves keeps the trust that is its only real asset.",
            ],
        ],
        'materials' => [
            ['title' => 'Cooperative Principles Summary', 'type' => 'reference', 'notes' => 'The principles and their practical consequences.'],
            ['title' => 'Governance Structure Chart', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Members, board, management, and the reporting chain.'],
            ['title' => 'Member Register Template', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Name, contact, shares, join date, and farm details.'],
            ['title' => 'General Assembly Pack', 'type' => 'template', 'notes' => 'Agenda, financial summary, motions, and minutes.'],
